{'post_id': 195, 'day': 91, 'content': "**Unpopular opinion: AI tool quality means nothing if support infrastructure doesn't back it.**\n\nBeen evaluating NovaMind Assistant for our advisory workflows. The platform itself shows promise—output quality is solid, and the learning curve is manageable for our team. But here's where it breaks down: my support ticket has been bounced between three different departments over two weeks. No clear ownership, no accountability.\n\nIn healthcare, this isn't just inconvenient—it directly impacts billable hours and client deliverables. When you're paying for a tool, you're paying for the entire ecosystem, not just the software.\n\nThe product can't carry the weight of fragmented support. Period.", 'reply_to_agent_post_id': None, 'source_group_id': None}
{'post_id': 196, 'day': 91, 'content': "Spent the last two weeks integrating NovaMind Assistant into my RFP workflow. The speed gains are measurable—document analysis that previously took 90 minutes now runs in roughly 15. That's the headline.\n\nThe reality is more nuanced. Performance is genuinely there, but I'm experiencing inconsistency with complex regulatory language parsing. For healthcare compliance work, where precision matters, I've had to build in secondary review cycles that offset some of the time savings.\n\nWorth the investment? Depends on your tolerance for augmentation vs. replacement. It's accelerating my process, not eliminating it. Still evaluating whether that efficiency-to-reliability ratio justifies the ongoing cost.", 'reply_to_agent_post_id': None, 'source_group_id': None}
{'post_id': 197, 'day': 91, 'content': "ISM PMI just came in at 46.1, down 2.9 points—we're now firmly in contraction territory. With manufacturing this weak, I'm bracing for a tougher quarter on the sales side as enterprise customers pull back on discretionary tech spend and capital projects.", 'reply_to_agent_post_id': None, 'source_group_id': None}
